# Bus Scribe — content settled with David, PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]

> bus_scribe is a general ComposeBus peer that, given an active `scribe_profile`, records every message matching that profile's namespace-set with a timestamp — capture, timestamp, persist, and nothing else. It never synthesizes or transforms. One dumb engine serves diagnostics, audit, replay and game-narrative alike, because the difference between those uses is entirely in which profile is active and what reads the output afterward, never in the engine. The output IS the public interface: an append-only JSONL stream a downstream consumer tails or folds with zero scribe involvement.

---

## 1. The engine/policy boundary — RATIFIED

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ENGINE] Dumb general engine — capture, timestamp, persist; it never synthesizes or transforms

- decision: bus_scribe is a general bus peer. Given an active `scribe_profile`, it records every message matching that profile's namespace-set, with a timestamp. Capture plus timestamp plus persist — nothing more.
- never-transforms: the engine NEVER synthesizes or interprets a message. It does not derive a new fact, does not summarize, does not join records across namespaces. What arrives is what is written.
- one-engine-many-uses: one scribe serves diagnostics, audit, replay, AND game-narrative. The difference between those uses lives entirely in which `scribe_profile` is active and in what reads the JSONL afterward — never in the engine.
- realizes: [DX-ENGINE-VS-POLICY] — the mechanism every deployment needs (capture-and-persist) is the engine; the mechanism only one deployment needs (what a game does with narrative events, what an operator does with a diagnostic replay) is policy, and it stays downstream, behind the JSONL seam.
- status: ratified
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-V2-DEFERRED], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-NARRATIVE-SOURCE]

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-V2-DEFERRED] Pluggable transformers and real-time livestream are explicitly OUT OF SCOPE for v1

- change: bus_scribe 2.0 — pluggable transformer modules (raw-to-semantic) and real-time livestream of a game unfolding — is deferred out of v1 scope, not designed here.
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: an early framing could have folded transformation and livestream into the scribe itself, since it already sees every matching message.
  reasoning: the engine/policy split ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ENGINE]) means transformation belongs downstream of the JSONL seam, not inside the engine that produces it.
  rejected_alternative: build raw-to-semantic transformer modules and a livestream path into bus_scribe now, as part of v1.
  quote: David — "may not be needed ever, or may be better handled by a different product that consumes the output of bus_scribe."
  invalidating_assumption: that a downstream consumer of the JSONL stream cannot cheaply provide transformation and livestream on its own. If a concrete v1 use case is blocked without them, this deferral is the first thing to re-open.
  implication: v1 ships with zero transform/livestream code in the engine; a bus_scribe-2.0 plugin or an entirely separate product may consume the JSONL output later, with zero scribe involvement either way.
- status: rejected-for-v1
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ENGINE], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OUTPUT]

---

## 2. The output contract — RATIFIED

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OUTPUT] The output IS the contract — an append-only JSONL stream of raw records

- decision: bus_scribe's output is an append-only **JSONL** stream. Each line is a raw record: `{ts, namespace, value, scope, source}`.
- public-interface: the stream is a public interface, not an implementation detail. A downstream product — a bus_scribe-2.0 plugin, or an entirely separate product — consumes it by `tail -f` for livestream or by folding the file for replay, with ZERO scribe involvement.
- consequence: because the contract is the file, not an API, any consumer that can read a JSONL file can consume bus_scribe output, forever, independent of what bus_scribe itself later becomes.
- status: ratified
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-V2-DEFERRED], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-SINK-SPLIT]

---

## 3. The sink split — RATIFIED

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-SINK-SPLIT] Live sink is JSONL; document_store CATALOGS completed transcripts — diagnostics vs narrative differ only in retention

- decision: the live sink is an append-only JSONL log, always. document_store CATALOGS completed transcripts for retrieval and replay — a completed transcript IS a document once it stops being written to.
- mechanism-is-one: diagnostics and narrative differ ONLY in retention/rotation policy, never in mechanism. A diagnostic stream and a game-narrative transcript are both JSONL, both written by the same engine, both catalogable once complete.
- reuses: document_store as the catalog of completed transcripts; bus_storage as the config-over-bus store the `scribe_profile` registry lives in ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE]).
- status: ratified
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OUTPUT], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET]

---

## 4. Narrative provenance — RATIFIED

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-NARRATIVE-SOURCE] Narrative comes from the GAME, not the scribe — the game owns the semantics

- decision: the game emits already-narrative events on `game/event/*`; it owns the semantics (it knows an attack happened). The scribe records those events raw, alongside whatever state namespaces the active profile names — it does not infer that a narrative event occurred.
- realizes: [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR] — the cheapest node that can fully own a behaviour owns all of it. The game is the node that knows its own semantics; the scribe is not, and must not be asked to reconstruct them from raw state.
- consequence: a bus_scribe transcript of a game session is exactly as narrative as the game chose to make its own bus traffic. The scribe adds nothing and loses nothing.
- status: ratified
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ENGINE]

---

## 5. The profile registry and activation — RATIFIED, with one architect-delegated call

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE] scribe_profile = {namespace-set, sink, format, retention} — defined in bus_storage, activated by a bus push

- decision: `scribe_profile` is a structure of `{namespace-set, sink, format, retention}`. It is DEFINED in **bus_storage**, at `scribe/profiles/<name>`, and ACTIVATED by a `_scribe/<name>/activate` push — announce-then-bind, control-is-a-peer, matching the `_device/<id>/transport` precedent in [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS].
- define-in-registry-activate-by-push: defining a profile and activating it are two separate actions on two separate namespaces. A profile can exist in the registry, unused, until something pushes an activation for it.
- architect-decision: the choice of bus_storage over document_store for WHERE a profile is defined is this architect's call, delegated by David — "document_store or bus_storage, you make the call" — not David's own stated preference. Rationale: a `scribe_profile` is CONFIG, and bus_storage is the config-over-bus store ([CXC-BUSSTORE-API-CONFIG]); document_store holds the OUTPUT documents (completed transcripts, [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-SINK-SPLIT]), not the config that produced them. Config-in, documents-out.
- inference-marker: the storage-location call above is architect-inference from David's delegation, per [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE] — it must be confirmed or corrected before it is cited as settled beyond "David delegated this choice."
- status: ratified
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OPEN-FORKS]
- registry-confirmed: David 2026-08-20 CONFIRMED bus_storage as the scribe_profile registry ("registry = bus storage, agreed") — the architect-inference flagged in inference-marker is now RATIFIED, no longer pending confirmation. The config-in/documents-out rationale stands as David's ratified call.

---

## 6. Deployment — SUPERSEDED

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-DEPLOYMENT] Default, always-present, per-tenant ComposeServer plugin — supersedes the standalone bare-Python fleet-service shape

- decision: bus_scribe is a DEFAULT (default_loaded, always-present) PER-TENANT ComposeServer plugin under [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT] / [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGINS] — NOT a standalone bare-Python fleet service. It runs inside the ComposeServer on COMPOSEIO_MQTT_BROKER alongside the document_store plugin, so the completed-transcript handoff stays local (co-location intent preserved). Per-tenant per [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] (a scribe must never cross the tenant membrane). It MAY pin its isolation floor to `subprocess` per [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR] (crash/disk-fault isolation), never `inprocess`. It declares a `requires:` storage floor + `ready:` predicate per [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR], so a deployment with no durable store PARKS it loudly rather than degrading silently.
- why-colocated: document_store is the catalog target for completed transcripts ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-SINK-SPLIT]); co-locating the scribe with its own sink keeps the completed-transcript handoff local rather than crossing the bus a second time. Under the plugin shape this co-location is now IN-PROCESS-SERVER (both plugins loaded into the same ComposeServer on COMPOSEIO_MQTT_BROKER) rather than sibling fleet processes, which strengthens the same locality intent.
- superseded-by: [DEC-BUS-SCRIBE-IS-DEFAULT-COMPOSESERVER-PLUGIN-2026-08-25]
- status: superseded
- provenance: david-ruling — the plugin shape, the default/always-present property, and the supersession of the standalone fleet-service deployment (`DEC-BUS-SCRIBE-IS-DEFAULT-COMPOSESERVER-PLUGIN-2026-08-25`, David 2026-08-25); architect-recommendation — the per-tenant scoping, the `subprocess` isolation-floor pin, and the `requires:`/`ready:` storage-floor consequence, per [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]

---

## 7. Storage budget — RATIFIED, first-class structural requirement

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET] A hard on-disk budget enforced IN CODE — not a knob, and it must be provably enforced

- decision: bus_scribe enforces a hard total on-disk storage budget IN CODE. (a private host name, not published) has a small drive today and needs a structural guarantee it will not be filled; a spinny disk is planned but not yet present.
- structural-not-a-knob: a limit any caller can exceed is not a limit. This requirement needs a negative-control test that proves eviction or refusal actually fires under pressure, not merely a config value that is trusted to be honored.
- per-class-policy: DIAGNOSTIC streams are high-volume and use ring-buffer semantics — rotate and evict-oldest within a per-profile cap. NARRATIVE transcripts are naturally session-bounded and small, so they do not need the same eviction pressure.
- loud-under-pressure: the budget publishes a loud bus alert under pressure (e.g. `_scribe/storage/pressure`) — observable, never a silent disk-full. This mirrors the loud-expiry discipline [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS] applies to its own TTL backstop.
- future-proof: the budget is a config VALUE, raised when the spinny disk lands — no code change required to raise the ceiling, only to enforce whatever ceiling is configured.
- status: ratified
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-SINK-SPLIT]

---

## 8. Open forks — NOT silently resolved

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OPEN-FORKS] Three points named so they are not silently decided — ALL THREE NOW RESOLVED

- retention-defaults: the retention/rotation DEFAULT values are open — the budget's total bytes, the per-profile caps, and the rotation size/time thresholds are none of them settled.
- eviction-policy-details: the exact eviction policy per class is open — evict-oldest (diagnostic) vs refuse-new-with-alert, and whether a completed narrative transcript is PRUNED from the live JSONL log once cataloged to document_store. This interacts directly with [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET], since document_store is ALSO on (a private host name, not published) and shares the same drive.
- activation-schema: the exact activation-intent shape and the `scribe_profile` schema itself (beyond the four named fields in [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE]) are open.
- open-for-ratify: retention-defaults and eviction-policy-details are RATIFIED by David 2026-08-20; activation-schema REMAINS OPEN. Retention/eviction DEFAULTS accepted as proposed ("retention/eviction defaults = fine"). Narrative transcripts ARE pruned from the live JSONL log once cataloged to document_store ("yes, prune narrative transcript from the live log once it's cataloged to the document store"), resolving the eviction-policy prune question; diagnostic streams keep evict-oldest per [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET]. Still open: the exact activation-intent shape and the scribe_profile schema beyond the four named fields.
- resolved-2026-08-25: activation-schema is now RESOLVED by this elevation's architect-ratified call — [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACTIVATION-SCHEMA]. The v1 `scribe_profile` schema is exactly the four named fields in [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE], no more (YAGNI); activation reuses the `_device/<id>/transport` config-over-bus precedent. No fork named in this node remains open as of this revision — a numeric value for retention-defaults' actual bytes/thresholds is a residual gap this elevation did NOT fill (the prose above records that David accepted "as proposed," but no proposed numbers are captured anywhere in this document); flagged for the architect, not fabricated here.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACTIVATION-SCHEMA]

