## 9. Activation schema — RESOLVED (architect-ratified, citing this elevation dispatch)

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACTIVATION-SCHEMA] v1 `scribe_profile` schema is EXACTLY four fields, no more until a use case demands it; activation reuses the `_device/<id>/transport` config-over-bus precedent

- decision: the v1 `scribe_profile` schema is EXACTLY the four named fields already stated in [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE] — `{namespace-set, sink, format, retention}` — and no more. YAGNI: no additional schema field is added speculatively; a fifth field is added only when a concrete use case demands it.
- field-shapes: (MUST, testable — the schema a builder implements)
  - namespace-set: list of str — bus namespace glob patterns this profile matches; the set the engine subscribes on activation.
  - sink: enum of diagnostic or narrative — selects the per-class retention/eviction policy [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET] applies (ring-buffer evict-oldest for diagnostic; session-bounded, pruned-on-catalog for narrative).
  - format: enum of jsonl only — v1 ships exactly one output format; the field exists for forward-compatibility, not because a second format is designed, per [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-V2-DEFERRED].
  - retention: a mapping of max-bytes (int), rotate-by (enum of size or time), and rotate-threshold (number) — the per-profile cap and rotation trigger, bounded within the total on-disk budget [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET] enforces.
- activation-shape: (MUST, testable) activation is a bus PUSH on `_scribe/<name>/activate`, carrying no payload beyond the activation intent itself — the profile's own fields are already resolved at `scribe/profiles/<name>` in bus_storage, so activation does not re-transmit them. This is the SAME announce-then-bind, control-is-a-peer shape [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]'s `_device/<id>/transport` precedent already established ([CXC-CONFIG-BUS-ADDRESSING], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS-STRATEGY]), applied to a profile name instead of a device id.
- rejects-undeclared-fields: (MUST, testable) a profile document defining a field outside the four named ones MUST be rejected at DEFINE time, never silently accepted as an ad hoc schema extension — mirroring [MODEL-ID]'s "a duplicate id is an error, not a merge" discipline applied to schema drift instead of identity.
- rationale: the schema stays at exactly these four fields because nothing in the settled design (engine/policy split, sink split, narrative provenance, budget) requires more, and adding speculative fields before a use case names them contradicts the deferred-v2 discipline [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-V2-DEFERRED] already applies to transformation and livestream. Reusing the `_device/<id>/transport` shape rather than inventing a new activation grammar is [DX-NARROW-WAIST] applied to this seam.
- change: this closes the third and final fork in [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OPEN-FORKS] — the exact activation-intent shape and the scribe_profile schema itself.
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: "[CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OPEN-FORKS] left the activation-intent shape and any schema fields beyond the four named ones open, pending ratify."
  reasoning: "YAGNI — the four fields already ratified in [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE] fully cover every ratified requirement in this document; no additional field is load-bearing for v1. Activation reuses the `_device/<id>/transport` precedent rather than a bespoke shape, keeping one config-over-bus idiom instead of two."
  rejected_alternative: "a richer schema (e.g. separate filter predicates beyond namespace-set, or a distinct activation payload carrying profile fields redundantly instead of by reference to the registry entry)."
  invalidating_assumption: "that no v1 use case needs a filter finer than a namespace-set, and that activation-by-name is sufficient (never needs an inline override of a defined profile's fields at activation time). If a concrete use case needs either, this schema reopens."
  implication: "a builder implements exactly this shape with no guessing; a fifth field is a NEW change block here, not a silent addition."
- status: resolved
- provenance: architect-ratified — this dispatch's explicit instruction, not David's verbatim words; recorded per [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OPEN-FORKS], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS-ADDRESSING], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

---

## 10. MCP tool-declaration surface — architect-recommendation

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-MCP-SURFACE] bus_scribe declares four MCP tools using [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]'s `McpToolDecl` shape, named for an Intelligence consumer

- requirement: (MUST, testable) bus_scribe exposes an MCP tool surface reusing [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]'s `McpToolDecl` shape — `name`, `description`, `input_model`, `direction`, `namespace_mapping`, `read_mode` (where applicable), `idempotent` — the SAME schema every ComposeServer plugin's tools derive from, so a builder or agent reading bus_scribe's tool list needs no second convention.
- naming-for-intelligence: (MUST, david-ruling) every tool name, parameter name, and description MUST make the tool's purpose unambiguous to an agent reading a tool list, favoring self-describing verbosity over human-API terseness, per [DEC-MCP-SURFACES-NAMED-FOR-INTELLIGENCE-2026-08-25] — David 2026-08-25, verbatim: "all API surfaces need to be named such that their purpose is clear to an Intelligence, even if much longer than a normal human-oriented API surface would be." The four tool names below already apply this.
- note-on-deployment-shape: (resolved 2026-08-25, cites [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-DEPLOYMENT] and `DEC-BUS-SCRIBE-IS-DEFAULT-COMPOSESERVER-PLUGIN-2026-08-25`) the tension this node previously flagged — this document's own dispatch phrasing ("built as a ComposeServer plugin") against [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-DEPLOYMENT]'s then-ratified standalone-fleet-service shape — is now DECIDED, not open. bus_scribe's four MCP tools JOIN the ComposeServer UMBRELLA: aggregated and exposed as `bus_scribe.{tool}` per [CXC-MCP-UMBRELLA-RATIFIED] / [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION], declared on bus_scribe's pydantic plugin manifest as the fourth derived artifact — NOT a standalone `type: mcp` connector per [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]. The four tool declarations below, their `input_model`s, and the naming-for-intelligence discipline ([DEC-MCP-SURFACES-NAMED-FOR-INTELLIGENCE-2026-08-25]) are UNCHANGED — only the aggregation/connector question resolves. BS-V10 stays valid but now realizes through the umbrella rather than through a standalone connector.
- tool-1: `define_named_scribe_profile_in_bus_storage_registry` — direction: write; input_model: `DefineScribeProfileArgs {profile_name: str, namespace_set: list[str], sink: enum(diagnostic, narrative), format: enum(jsonl), retention: {max_bytes: int, rotate_by: enum(size, time), rotate_threshold: number}}`; namespace_mapping: `{publishes: ["scribe/profiles/{profile_name}"]}`; idempotent: true; description: "Define or replace a named scribe_profile in the bus_storage registry. Does not activate it — a profile can exist, unused, until a separate activation call names it. The four fields are exactly [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACTIVATION-SCHEMA]'s ratified schema, no more."
- tool-2: `activate_scribe_profile_by_name` — direction: write; input_model: `ActivateScribeProfileArgs {profile_name: str}`; namespace_mapping: `{publishes: ["_scribe/{profile_name}/activate"]}`; idempotent: false (unconfirmed whether re-activating an already-active profile is safe to retry; a builder question, not fabricated here); description: "Activate a previously-defined scribe_profile by name, pushing `_scribe/<name>/activate` so bus_scribe begins recording that profile's namespace-set. MUST fail loudly, named, if no profile with that name exists in the registry — never activates a fabricated profile."
- tool-3: `list_defined_scribe_profiles_in_registry` — direction: read; read_mode: query; input_model: `ListScribeProfilesArgs {}` (no fields); namespace_mapping: resolves via bus_storage's own registry-listing seam (see resolution-note below — the exact mechanism is undetermined here); idempotent: true; description: "List every scribe_profile currently defined in the bus_storage registry, independent of which (if any) are currently active. Read-only."
- tool-4: `list_completed_scribe_transcripts_in_document_store` — direction: read; read_mode: query; input_model: `ListScribeTranscriptsArgs {profile_name: optional str}`; namespace_mapping: resolves via document_store's own catalog-listing seam (see resolution-note below); idempotent: true; description: "List completed (cataloged) scribe transcripts recorded in document_store — the sink named in [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-SINK-SPLIT]. Read-only."
- resolution-note: (architect-recommendation, an open point this elevation surfaces rather than resolves) tools 3 and 4 read from bus_storage's and document_store's own listing/catalog seams, which this document's `boundary` explicitly does not respecify. Whether that seam is reachable as a `query`-mode bus round-trip (fitting [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]'s standard table-driven decode) or is a direct REST proxy inside bus_scribe's own MCP connector code (outside that standard mechanism) is UNDETERMINED here and is a builder decision against bus_storage's/document_store's actual APIs, not fabricated by this spec.
- reuse-by-reference: the MCP JSON-RPC tool-list/tool-call wire format itself, and the JSON-schema-from-`input_model` derivation, are owned by [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION] and [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN] and are not respecified here.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION], [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-DEPLOYMENT], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACTIVATION-SCHEMA], [DEC-MCP-SURFACES-NAMED-FOR-INTELLIGENCE-2026-08-25]

