## 11. Acceptance — machine signal + glass gate

### [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACCEPTANCE] The engine's two-tier verdict — a machine signal AND, for every failure class human eyes can catch that a narrow test cannot, an explicit human/glass gate

- definition: per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE] and the WS/MCP precedent ([CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE], [CXC-MCP-ACCEPTANCE]), machine-green alone never accepts this engine — a storage budget that is merely a trusted config value, or a narrative transcript pruned before it is safely cataloged, are exactly the failure classes a narrow unit test can pass while the real deployment is unsafe. [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS] applies throughout this table.
- requirement: BS-V7 (the storage-budget negative control) is the FIRM gate of this table — (a private host name, not published) has a small drive TODAY and shares it with document_store ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OPEN-FORKS]'s eviction-policy-details note); a budget that is enforced only by convention and never proven to fire under pressure is UNSAFE to ship, because a full disk on (a private host name, not published) degrades document_store too, not only bus_scribe. This mirrors [CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE]'s WS-V4 / [CXC-MCP-ACCEPTANCE]'s MCP-V4 firm-gate discipline exactly.

| ID | Requirement | verify-machine | verify-human (glass) |
|----|-------------|-----------------|------------------------|
| BS-V1 | Dumb engine — capture, timestamp, persist; never synthesizes or transforms ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ENGINE]) | a fixture publishes N messages across >=2 namespaces under an active profile; the JSONL output contains exactly N records, each byte-identical in `value` to the published payload, with no derived/joined/summarized field ever present | none required |
| BS-V2 | Output is append-only JSONL, `{ts, namespace, value, scope, source}` per line ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OUTPUT]) | `tail -f` and a full-file fold both parse every line as one JSON object carrying exactly those five keys; a consumer process with zero scribe-specific code parses the stream | none required |
| BS-V3 | Live sink is JSONL always; document_store catalogs a transcript once it stops being written to ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-SINK-SPLIT]) | closing/rotating a live JSONL stream triggers a document_store catalog write within a bounded time; the catalog entry references the same records; a still-open (in-progress) stream is NEVER visible in document_store's catalog | operator confirms a completed session's transcript is retrievable from document_store shortly after the session ends |
| BS-V4 | Narrative provenance stays with the game; the engine never infers a narrative event from raw state ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-NARRATIVE-SOURCE]) | a fixture publishes raw state changes on a profile's namespace-set WITHOUT any `game/event/*` message; the resulting JSONL contains zero synthesized narrative records — every record's `value` matches a message that was actually published | none required — machine-observable by construction, the engine has no narrative-inference code path to exercise |
| BS-V5 | `scribe_profile` DEFINED in bus_storage at `scribe/profiles/<name>`, ACTIVATED by a separate `_scribe/<name>/activate` push ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PROFILE], [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACTIVATION-SCHEMA]) | a profile defined but never activated records nothing; the SAME profile activated by name begins recording within one bus round-trip; activating an undefined name fails loudly, named, never silently no-ops | none required |
| BS-V6 | Runs as a default_loaded, per-tenant ComposeServer plugin, co-located with the document_store plugin on COMPOSEIO_MQTT_BROKER ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-DEPLOYMENT]) | loaded via the ComposeServer plugin loader as a default plugin, one instance per tenant bus; the completed-transcript handoff to the document_store plugin stays in-server/local (never crosses the bus a second time) | operator confirms bus_scribe is a LOADED PLUGIN (not a standalone process) in the ComposeServer on COMPOSEIO_MQTT_BROKER |
| BS-V7 | A hard total on-disk storage budget is enforced IN CODE, never merely a trusted config value ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-BUDGET]) | NEGATIVE CONTROL, required: a fixture drives a diagnostic-class profile's disk usage up to its configured cap; the write path evicts-oldest (or refuses, per class) BEFORE exceeding the cap, and `_scribe/storage/pressure` publishes; a PAIRED control run with the SAME fixture and the cap set to 10x the payload size must NOT evict, refuse, or publish the pressure alert — proving enforcement is actually gated by the configured value, not an unconditional/always-firing stub | operator watches (a private host name, not published)'s disk usage during a sustained load test and confirms it never exceeds the configured budget, with a visible bus alert as it approaches the cap |
| BS-V8 | Diagnostic streams evict-oldest under a per-profile ring-buffer cap; narrative transcripts are pruned from the live log once cataloged to document_store ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-OPEN-FORKS]) | a diagnostic-class fixture confirms oldest-record eviction under pressure; a narrative-class fixture confirms the live JSONL entry is removed within a bounded time after its document_store catalog write succeeds, and NOT before | none required |
| BS-V9 | v1 `scribe_profile` schema is exactly the four named fields; activation reuses the `_device/<id>/transport` config-over-bus shape ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-ACTIVATION-SCHEMA]) | a profile document defining a fifth, undeclared field is rejected at DEFINE time, never silently accepted as new schema; the activation payload carries no profile-field data | none required |
| BS-V10 | bus_scribe declares its MCP tools using the `McpToolDecl` shape, named for an Intelligence consumer ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-MCP-SURFACE]) | tool-list JSON-schema for each of the four declared tools matches its `input_model` field-for-field; every tool name/description is self-describing without external documentation, reviewed against [DEC-MCP-SURFACES-NAMED-FOR-INTELLIGENCE-2026-08-25] | a builder implementing bus_scribe's MCP surface needs no question answered outside this table and [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION] |
| BS-V11 | Placement recorded, not silent ([CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-PLACEMENT]) | the spec carries the `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` node + the filename-check property | n/a — structural |

- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
- see-also: [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE], [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS], [CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE], [CXC-MCP-ACCEPTANCE]

